[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  friday august 19th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  if the browser is the main gateway to  the internet than the current main  gatekeepers or google and apple  together google's chrome and apple  safari account for more than 80 percent  of the web browser market  and that has regulators and rivals  raising questions about whether  anti-trust controls or greater consumer  protections are needed  joining us to discuss this is our tech  reporter miles krupa hi miles thanks for  being here thanks zoe  miles can you just lay out in a little  more detail what exactly regulators and  rivals are claiming that these two  browser operators are doing that's  anti-competitive because you know just  being big isn't necessarily  anti-competitive  that is true  the bone that people have to pick with  apple in particular  is that apple forces  other browser makers to  use what they call a browser engine that  is developed by apple  to  build browsers on apple  devices  so  google has its own browser engine  firefox has its own browser engine  however  when they want to offer a browser on  iphones they have to build them using  safari's webkit so that's the big  contention with apple  with google  we've heard from multiple competitors in  search  and other areas that  google has used  chrome to basically make it more  difficult for these competitors to  distribute their products to come across  something like duckduckgo a  privacy-focused search engine to google  you have to go to a browser and you have  to find it and google  has about two-thirds of the browser  market so  if google makes it difficult to download  something like a browser extension for  duckduckgo  then you know duckduckgo would say  that's impeding  their ability to distribute their  products  how have google and apple responded to  those claims  so the first thing to note is both  companies will say  you know this market is very  very competitive  it's easy to switch between browsers  it's easy to make a browser a default on  an operating system  you know people don't have to use chrome  they don't have to use safari  so that's their first point  and  then beyond that apple has all sorts of  reasonings for why they have this webkit  requirement  the foremost being  security that they believe it is the  most secure  browser engine and is sort of critical  to maintaining  security and privacy on apple mobile  devices other people dispute that but  that's a much longer conversation  and then  with google they maintain  that they participate in all sorts of  web standards groups and that they don't  do anything anti-competitive with the  browser extensions  and basically that chrome is one because  it's a better browser  it does seem like lawmakers though are  kind of taking note of what rivals are  saying and are looking at kind of the  the scale of the control that these  companies have over the browser market  so what kind of actions are lawmakers or  regulators around the world starting to  take  right it's still early days the uk  competition and markets authority  is doing an investigation into mobile  browsers that is really squarely focused  on apple and google  and there could be all sorts of remedies  that come out of that they could ask  that apple  stop  this requirement that other browsers use  webkit on mobile devices they could ask  google to  potentially  give options for other browsers when you  open up chrome  they could do all sorts of things to try  to promote more competition in the  market  what's the competition like now i mean  if if so many of us are using chrome and  safari you know listeners might not  really be aware of what their other  options are  yeah a lot of the alternatives are  really focused on privacy so you know  it's no secret that data is really  important to google and google collects  a lot of data on its users through  chrome  so some of the alternatives  really market themselves as privacy  friendly not collecting user data  another one i spoke to called the  browser company has made a browser  called arc  that is really trying to put a whole new  user experience on the browser  where it's basically saying  we do all of our work in the browser  these days we don't use our desktops so  the browser needs to reflect that and  things like tab overcrowding  that can be streamlined through a few  different design choices tab  overcrowding is certainly something that  i am guilty of i think there's like 30  tabs open in my browser right now  i want to take a step back for a second  how did chrome and safari become so big  and and so important to our internet  experience  yeah well safari predated chrome it was  introduced in 2003  and interestingly enough from the  beginning it had this partnership with  google where google was the default  search engine in safari so that was a  really important distribution  channel for google  and eventually google decided in 2008  that it also needed its own browser  and what that's really done for google  over time  is  it's been  really the key  portal for people  interacting with google search you know  google chrome now has  about two-thirds of internet activity  worldwide  and it's really a simple layout to the  point that they combined the url bar  where you type in websites  and the search bar where you can ask  google  questions about all sorts of things  right  so  by tying those two things together  they've really trained users  to go to chrome and to go to that search  bar  when they have a question about anything  you know this idea that a web browser  has become too big or might be giving a  big tech company an upper hand isn't  entirely new in the 1990s you know  people complain that microsoft was  giving internet explorer an unfair  competitive advantage by bundling it  with its operating system  so why are regulators kind of back at  this point now looking at browsers again  and kind of what is their impetus to to  kind of keep going and pushing this  forward  you know browsers sit at this really  interesting  intersection where  they are really important to the way we  experience the web and really important  in  determining how  all sorts of developers create  applications for the web  so i think there's a growing realization  that as more people are spending time  online  as more people are interacting with  applications that are native to the web  that these browsers are really  important not just for like these  companies as big businesses  but for this open web economy that's  sprung up over the past decade plus  all right that's our reporter miles  krupa thanks for joining us miles thanks  again zoey  and that's it for tech news briefing  this week our producer is julie chang  our supervising producer is chris  sinsley our executive producer is kateri  yoakum and i'm your host  zoe thomas  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  [Music]  you

